meta {
  name: login
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "{{adminEmail}}",
    "password": "{{process.env.BRUNO_ADMIN_PASSWORD}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  // Bruno runs this for every response, not only 2xx. Unguarded, a refused
  // login overwrites a working token with undefined and every later request
  // answers 401 — with the real cause two requests upstream, which is the
  // confusion this collection exists to remove.
  if (res.getStatus() === 200) {
    bru.setVar("accessToken", res.body.access_token);
    bru.setVar("refreshToken", res.body.refresh_token);
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.token_type: eq Bearer
  res.body.expires_in: gt 0
}

docs {
  Run this first. It stores accessToken and refreshToken, and every request
  with `auth: inherit` picks accessToken up from collection.bru.

  expires_in tracks AUTH_ACCESS_TTL, 15m by default, so it is asserted as a
  shape rather than as 900. Pinning the number would turn "I lowered the TTL
  to watch expiry" into a red assertion pointing at the collection.

  Login answers without X-RateLimit-* headers, unlike every other endpoint.
  A remaining count next to a refused login is a progress meter for whoever
  is guessing. Only Retry-After survives.

  Two limits apply: 10 attempts per IP and 5 per email address, both per 15
  minutes. A full collection run spends six of the ten, so running it twice
  in a row runs out partway through the second. A 429 is the limiter, not a
  broken service; errors/folder.bru gives the command to clear it.
}
